{"reference":"1 Corinthians 8","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter begins with a disputed claim about knowledge, then places it beside love. Paul affirms the confession of one God and one Lord, but he does not let correct ideas about idols settle the practical question by themselves. The issue is carried forward into prior association, conscience, visible temple dining, and the possible harm done to another person.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 8:1-13","1 Corinthians 8:4-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_008_5d8cf3c05e6c1b056ea23942"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Food Question Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"“Food sacrificed to idols” should not be treated as one undifferentiated situation. First Corinthians distinguishes dining in an idol temple, meat sold in a market, and meals in private homes. This distinction keeps the chapter from becoming either a timeless diet rule or a purely abstract dispute about what idols are.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 8:1-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_008_669bcef396da5b32b47ae5fa"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"In verses 7–13, conscience is connected with prior idol association, weakness, defilement, and awareness of another person. It is therefore not simply an infallible private feeling, nor a tool for controlling someone else. The chapter’s “weak” person need not be intellectually inferior; the concern is the social and personal force of an action that another person sees and may be drawn to imitate.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 8:7-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_008_7c6507afe34d7b5ae213da20"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Verse 8 denies that eating or abstaining creates spiritual rank. Yet this does not make food choices inconsequential: some people continue to eat with an idol in view because of prior association, and Paul turns attention from the right-holder’s correctness to the vulnerable sibling. His final conditional promise of restraint presents restraint as the responsibility of the person with liberty, not as forced conformity by the vulnerable person.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 8:11-13","1 Corinthians 8:7","1 Corinthians 8:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_008_6c2702df9385a5d197714f18","reader_path_1_corinthians_008_1c86654f2d125459b8b60cbc"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Discussion","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The food discussion continues through chapters 9–11, where different meal settings and questions are distinguished. Reading these cases together cautions against flattening cultic dining, market food, private hospitality, assembly supper, table withdrawal, or other food disputes into a single rule.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:1-33","1 Corinthians 11:1-34","1 Corinthians 9:1-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_008_de2f76438643de1e06506059"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}